@prefix cd: <https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/ontology/> .
@prefix ex: <https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/resource/> .

# Authored continuous prose, not a concatenation of slide bullets. Charts, tables,
# mathematical expressions and their legends are projected from the same scenes.
<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/specifications/level4-script-narration> {
  ex:l4-scene-summary-gap cd:narration "A mean and a standard deviation do not determine a distribution. The four constructed detector-response series below each contain 100 observations and share an empirical mean of 50 a.u. and a sample SD of 10 a.u. Nevertheless, their uniform, normal-like, right-skewed and two-cluster patterns differ markedly. These are deterministic synthetic illustrations, not measurements or fitted population models.\n\nThis chapter introduces complementary descriptions of the same observations. Order statistics retain rank information; histograms aggregate local frequencies; ECDFs accumulate frequencies without bins; moments summarize selected aspects of location, variation and shape. None replaces inspection of the observations and their analytical context."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-data-concept cd:narration "A sample consists of individual observations with values, identities and measurement units. The notation $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$ indexes these observations, and $n$ is their count. Identical measured values still belong to distinct observations and must not be silently deduplicated.\n\nThe small five-reading example is intended for hand calculation. The larger analytical applications use at least 50 observations per series. A presentation order is not necessarily acquisition order; sorting changes positions, not what was measured."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-order-notation-concept cd:narration "An order statistic is an observation at a specified position after sorting by measured value. Parenthesized subscripts distinguish ordered positions, such as $x_{(1)}$, from the original observation indices. The minimum and maximum occupy the first and last ordered positions.\n\nKeep ties and observation identities when sorting. The two readings equal to 10.0 mg/L in the worked example remain two contributions to every count and empirical average. Ordered positions are a tool for computing rank-based summaries, not evidence about a time sequence."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-range-concept cd:narration "The sample range is the largest observed value minus the smallest. It describes the full observed span in the original measurement unit and is straightforward to calculate from ordered observations. A range of zero means all observed values coincide.\n\nThe range describes this sample, not the support of an unknown population distribution. Its reliance on two endpoints makes it sensitive to extremes and sample size, motivating additional descriptions of the interior observations."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-range-applications cd:narration "Observed span can be useful when checking whether repeated measurements remain within an operating region or when describing the variation among river grab samples. The accompanying table calculates the same endpoint difference for each synthetic analytical series.\n\nInterpret the result with its measurement conditions. A large river range may represent environmental variation rather than instrumental imprecision, whereas a repeated-measurement range concerns the operations actually repeated. A descriptive span is not an acceptance rule or a confidence interval."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-range-limits cd:narration "Different arrangements of observations can share the same endpoints and therefore the same range. A sample concentrated near the centre and one concentrated near the extremes may have equal spans while supporting different descriptions of their typical values.\n\nAn isolated extreme can also change the range greatly without changing most readings. Retain the value and investigate its origin rather than treating influence on a statistic as sufficient grounds for deletion. Interior rank summaries help expose what the range omits."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-quartiles-concept cd:narration "Quartiles mark the first quarter, middle and third quarter of an ordered distribution. For observed data, their numerical values depend on an explicit quantile convention. Here all quartiles use linear interpolation between adjacent ordered observations.\n\nThe median is the second quartile. A quartile need not be an observed value, because interpolation can locate it between measurements. This distinction matters when interpreting boundaries and comparing software output."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-quartiles-applications cd:narration "Quartiles describe the lower, middle and upper parts of an ordered sample without depending solely on its two endpoints. The river and effluent applications retain 50 original observations each, with their own concentration scales and synthetic analytical contexts.\n\nRead the three reported quartiles together. Their spacing indicates whether the central ranks are evenly spread or concentrated more strongly on one side of the median. These empirical landmarks do not by themselves identify a population distribution or an analytical cause."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-quartiles-limits cd:narration "Quartiles omit much of the information in a sample. Two series can preserve the same central ranks while having substantially different upper extremes. Rounded observations and ties can also place many values on a quartile boundary.\n\nA central-rank summary therefore cannot settle a question about tail magnitude or the frequency of a rare extreme. Retain the raw observations and use a view that corresponds to the scientific question. The linked examples deliberately expose information that a quartile triplet suppresses."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-quantiles-concept cd:narration "A quantile extends the quartile idea to a chosen cumulative proportion $p$. Linear interpolation locates a fractional rank and combines its adjacent ordered observations. The displayed expression defines the rank, its integer part and the interpolation weight.\n\nThe convention and endpoint conditions are part of the result. Reporting a percentile without stating how it was computed can make apparently conflicting software outputs difficult to interpret, particularly for small samples."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-quantiles-applications cd:narration "Choose a quantile to match the intended descriptive question. An upper percentile can summarize an observed concentration region or a blank-equivalent response level without reducing the discussion to the maximum alone. The table calculates the requested quantiles from the original synthetic observations.\n\nSuch a sample quantile is not automatically a detection limit, a regulatory threshold or a validated decision boundary. Those interpretations require additional analytical assumptions and evidence beyond interpolation of ordered values."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-quantiles-limits cd:narration "High sample quantiles can depend on very few observations, especially in a small dataset. The two blank-equivalent examples show how changing an upper reading affects a high-percentile calculation. This is a property of the finite data and the stated interpolation convention.\n\nA precisely printed decimal does not imply a precisely known population percentile. Check sample size, measurement resolution and the purpose of the quantile before interpreting it as a stable analytical characteristic."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-iqr-concept cd:narration "The interquartile range, IQR, is the third quartile minus the first. It measures the width between the outer central quartiles in the original unit. Throughout this lecture, both boundaries use the same linear interpolation.\n\nThe IQR is a width, not a radius about the median. In a skewed sample, the interval between the first and third quartiles need not be symmetric around that median. This central-rank description is useful precisely because endpoint magnitudes do not determine it."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-iqr-applications cd:narration "For recovery measurements, the IQR can compare the central spread of extraction procedures at a common fortification level. The synthetic extraction and control series each contain 50 observations. Their table and point diagram preserve the recovery scale, so widths can be compared directly.\n\nThe narrower extraction series has the smaller central-rank width. For a control series, however, a narrow marginal distribution does not establish stability over time. Run order and the conditions of replication remain relevant even when the central spread is small."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-iqr-limits cd:narration "The IQR intentionally limits the influence of extreme endpoint magnitudes. That is useful for a robust central summary but means a long tail can change while the IQR remains unchanged. The paired river examples illustrate this limitation.\n\nA robust summary does not make tail observations scientifically unimportant. When investigating contamination, exposure or instrument saturation, the extremes and their origins may be the main question rather than a nuisance to summarize away."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-mad-concept cd:narration "The raw median absolute deviation, MAD, is the median of the absolute distances from the sample median. First determine the centre, then calculate each absolute distance and take their median. The result has the original measurement unit.\n\nThis lecture uses raw MAD, corresponding to R's constant equal to one. A normal-consistency scaling factor defines a different numerical quantity and must not be silently mixed into comparisons. Raw MAD is a typical median distance, not a universal probability interval."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-mad-applications cd:narration "The repeatability and spike examples compare a robust median-distance summary with the visible observations. Retaining all 50 observations in each application shows what the summary responds to and what it intentionally de-emphasizes.\n\nAn extreme reading can alter a mean-based dispersion measure substantially while having little effect on the median and raw MAD. This is a descriptive contrast, not a diagnosis that the extreme is erroneous. Analytical provenance determines whether a value represents contamination, a different condition or valid variation."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-mad-limits cd:narration "A nonconstant rounded sample can have raw MAD equal to zero. If enough observations coincide with the median, the middle absolute distance is zero even though other measurements differ. The range and point diagram retain this otherwise hidden variation.\n\nInterpret zero MAD with the measurement resolution and ties in mind. It does not establish absence of variation, and dividing another quantity by a zero robust scale would be undefined."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-spread-normal cd:narration "SD, IQR and raw MAD answer related but different questions. SD describes squared deviations from the mean and is useful in variance-based models; IQR describes a width between central ranks; raw MAD describes a typical absolute distance from the median. Their sensitivities and limitations differ.\n\nThe diagram repeats the same 100 normal-like observations in three columns. It draws mean plus or minus sample SD, the quartile interval, and median plus or minus raw MAD. Their actual closed-interval counts are 68, 50 and 50. The labels are counted from the sample, not inferred from universal percentage rules."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-spread-skewed cd:narration "The right-skewed sample has the same empirical mean of 50 a.u. and sample SD of 10 a.u. as the preceding normal-like sample, yet mean plus or minus SD now contains 85 of the 100 observations. A shared SD does not imply shared interval coverage.\n\nThe quartile interval is not centred on the median, and raw MAD remains a median-distance summary. Use robust central descriptions when appropriate, while preserving the long tail when its magnitude is analytically relevant. None of these displayed bands is a confidence interval."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-box-construction cd:narration "A boxplot combines quartiles, a median and observed whisker endpoints into one compact rank-based display. Here the box boundaries are linearly interpolated quartiles. Inner fences at one and a half IQRs beyond the quartiles identify observations to draw separately.\n\nWhiskers stop at the most extreme observations within those fences; they do not stop at the numerical fence itself. Separately drawn points remain part of the dataset and every subsequent calculation. A flag is not a decision to delete a measurement."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-box-applications cd:narration "Boxplots can compare recovery distributions across preparations or control conditions on the same measurement scale. The three synthetic 50-observation applications retain their original values and use the same quartile and fence conventions.\n\nRead centre, central width and observed tails together rather than judging quality from box width alone. A narrow box can be displaced from a relevant reference, and a broad box may reflect deliberately varied conditions rather than poor instrumental repeatability."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-box-limits cd:narration "A boxplot summarizes marginal ranks, not acquisition order. The same values in a different run sequence have the same quartiles and whiskers, even if their order suggests a different stability question. The paired examples preserve this distinction.\n\nInspect run charts or other order-aware evidence when time, drift or changing conditions matter. The compact box representation should not be used to infer properties that it does not encode."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-box-extremes cd:narration "The synthetic river example contains 49 values from 0.5 to 24.5 mg/L and one value at 90 mg/L. The upper fence is 37.25 mg/L, whereas the upper whisker ends at the observed 24.5 mg/L. The 90 mg/L observation is drawn separately.\n\nThis separation makes the observation visible without removing it. Investigate identity, sampling and instrument records before interpreting the cause. A fence rule describes a positional flag, not a validated analytical error criterion."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-box-symmetric cd:narration "The normal-like synthetic responses have a median near the centre of the box and broadly comparable distances on either side. This is consistent with central symmetry of the observed sample. All 100 observations remain visible alongside the summary.\n\nA symmetric-looking box is not a normality test. Tail detail and local occupancy require additional information, which is why the point display and subsequent histograms are retained."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-box-skewed cd:narration "The right-skewed detector responses have a longer upper box half and upper tail. Their median is approximately 46.92 a.u., while the constructed sample mean is 50 a.u. The box and individual points provide complementary descriptions of this asymmetry.\n\nSeveral large values may be legitimate tail observations. The shape alone does not identify contamination, a mixture or another analytical cause, and a boxplot flag does not settle that question."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-box-bimodal cd:narration "The two-cluster synthetic sample places most responses near 40 and 60 a.u., with few near the median of 50 a.u. A single box spans the gap and can conceal this two-region occupancy.\n\nA centre can therefore lie in a region containing almost no observations. Retaining the points and inspecting a histogram prevents the compact rank summary from being mistaken for a complete description of shape."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-box-uniform cd:narration "The uniform illustration contains 100 evenly spaced constructed responses. Its box is symmetric, yet the occupancy across its range is approximately flat rather than bell-shaped. It shares the same empirical mean and sample SD as the other constructed shapes.\n\nSymmetry, normality and unimodality are different claims. A boxplot can support a discussion of central ranks without establishing any particular population distribution family."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-box-lab cd:narration "The first executable laboratory compares recovery samples with ggplot2 boxplots. Run the code in the slide view, inspect the reported boundaries and observed whiskers, and compare them with the accompanying analytical cases. The script retains the full editable source for reading and printing.\n\nChange the designated extreme observation and predict which features should move. The output should be interpreted using the stated quartile and fence conventions; first execution may obtain ggplot2 through the existing local R runtime."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-intro cd:narration "A histogram groups numerical observations into adjacent measurement intervals. With equal-width bins, a bar's height can represent the number of observations in that interval. The bars are not unrelated categories and are not individual measured signal heights.\n\nThis lecture uses left-closed, right-open intervals, except that the final upper endpoint is included. Every original observation is counted exactly once. The axes and boundary convention are essential parts of the representation."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-reading cd:narration "The normal-like synthetic control responses accumulate most strongly near 50 a.u., with fewer readings toward either tail. Reading centre, observed span and approximate symmetry together provides more information than reporting only a mean and SD.\n\nA taller bar means more observations within its stated bin. It does not mean a higher concentration per observation and does not establish an exact population density or a fitted distribution family."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-skewed cd:narration "The right-skewed histogram has a dense lower-response region and a sparse upper tail. Its constructed mean of 50 a.u. exceeds its median of approximately 46.92 a.u. The bar pattern makes this imbalance easier to inspect than a single central summary.\n\nPossible analytical explanations require information about sampling, matrix, batches and measurement operations. The histogram contains no acquisition order, and a tail observation is not automatically an error."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-bimodal cd:narration "The two-cluster sample produces occupied regions near 40 and 60 a.u. with a sparse middle. A mean of 50 a.u. does not communicate this separation and may describe an atypical location for an individual reading.\n\nCheck relevant matrix, batch or method labels before assigning a cause to the apparent groups. A histogram suggests questions about subgroups; it does not identify their mechanism or statistical significance by itself."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-2d-intro cd:narration "A two-dimensional histogram counts paired observations in rectangular cells formed by one bin on each measurement axis. Each of the 60 synthetic sensor observations retains its concentration and detector response together. Independently sorting or re-pairing the coordinates would change the evidence.\n\nCell numbers and colours encode counts, with empty cells representing zero. They do not encode response magnitude. Read both numerical axes and the count scale when interpreting the pattern."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-2d-reading cd:narration "Occupied cells form an upward band in the synthetic concentration-response example. Higher concentrations tend to accompany higher responses in these constructed pairs. Aggregation makes the broad association visible while hiding the exact positions within each cell.\n\nThe image is not a fitted calibration, proof of linearity or a causal conclusion. A model-based analytical assessment requires additional assumptions and checks of the original paired observations."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-too-coarse cd:narration "One bin from 30 to 70 a.u. correctly contains all 100 readings of the two-cluster sample. It also removes the visible gap between the occupied regions. Correct counting alone does not ensure that a view answers the scientific question.\n\nFor distinguishing the two regions, this binning is too coarse. The data have not changed; only the chosen aggregation has hidden their structure."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-too-fine cd:narration "At the opposite extreme, 400 bins of width 0.1 a.u. leave most bins empty or containing one observation. Individual gaps and spikes dominate the picture. They are not evidence for hundreds of distinct subpopulations.\n\nMore bins do not automatically provide a more informative summary. Choose a useful level of aggregation with attention to sample size, measurement resolution and the question being asked."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-sensible-width cd:narration "Ten bins of width 4 a.u. reveal both occupied regions of the constructed sample without reducing every observation to an isolated spike. The sparse middle remains visible. This provides a useful view for the current question.\n\nThe width is not universally optimal. Compare neighbouring choices and retain the instrument's resolution and analytical context rather than treating one visually appealing histogram as uniquely correct."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-origin cd:narration "Shifting bin boundaries while retaining their width changes which observations are grouped together. The resulting bar heights can change even though every measured value is identical to the preceding example. The covering grid still counts all 100 observations.\n\nBroad features that survive reasonable width and origin changes are more credible than an isolated boundary-dependent spike. When comparing groups, use common edges and compatible normalization."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-rules cd:narration "Freedman–Diaconis, Scott and Sturges provide explicit starting choices for histogram binning. Freedman–Diaconis uses the IQR, Scott uses sample SD, and Sturges proposes a bin count from sample size. The formula legend explains every quantity and the expanded names.\n\nThese rules do not replace inspection of shape or knowledge of measurement resolution. Degenerate data can produce no positive width, and converting a proposed width into a covering grid may add a boundary bin."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-rule-example cd:narration "For the 100 normal-like responses, the Freedman–Diaconis width is approximately 5.75 a.u., Scott's approximately 7.54 a.u., and Sturges suggests eight bins over the observed span. The interactive slide allows comparison with neighbouring widths and shifted origins.\n\nThe script starts from the authored histogram, with all 100 observations counted. These are starting rules rather than scientific verdicts. A covering grid, rounding and the permitted control range should always be disclosed when comparing a proposal with the actual edges."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-variable-width cd:narration "Unequal measurement widths require a different height interpretation. A bar's area, not its height alone, must represent the sample fraction in that bin. Relative-frequency density divides the bin count by both sample size and bin width.\n\nMultiplying density by width recovers the bin's observed fraction, and the areas sum to one. Raw count heights with unequal widths would confound occupancy with the area assigned to each interval."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-unequal-sort cd:narration "A small constructed sample makes adaptive boundaries calculable by hand: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10 and 20 mg/L. We choose four bins using the minimum, first quartile, median, third quartile and maximum. This is one explicit data-dependent choice, not a unique correct partition.\n\nThe fractional rank for linear interpolation is computed from sample size and cumulative proportion. Interpolation uses the two adjacent ordered measurements. The larger applications follow after this eight-value calculation."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-unequal-interpolate cd:narration "With eight observations, the fractional ranks for the quartiles are 2.75, 4.5 and 6.25. Interpolating gives boundaries 2.75, 4.5 and 7 mg/L. Together with the endpoints, the edges are 1, 2.75, 4.5, 7 and 20 mg/L.\n\nThe resulting widths are 1.75, 1.75, 2.5 and 13 mg/L. Under the stated endpoint convention, each interval contains two observations. Ties can prevent equal counts or create repeated boundaries; merge repeated edges rather than creating zero-width bins."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-unequal-density cd:narration "Every interval in the eight-value example contains two of eight observations, so each area must equal one quarter. Dividing that fraction by widths of 1.75, 1.75, 2.5 and 13 gives heights approximately 0.14286, 0.14286, 0.10 and 0.01923 per mg/L.\n\nThe last bar is low because its interval is wide, not because it contains fewer observations. Height times width equals 0.25 for each bar, and their total area is one."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-variable-construction cd:narration "The larger adaptive example applies the same minimum/quartile/maximum construction to 100 synthetic right-skewed responses. Its four intervals each contain 25 observations, but their measurement widths differ substantially.\n\nNarrow intervals resolve the dense lower-response region while a wide interval summarizes the sparse upper tail. Relative-frequency density preserves the fraction through area. The explicit edges and linear interpolation make the calculation reproducible."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-variable-use cd:narration "Adaptive widths can retain detail in a dense region without producing many almost-empty tail bins. This benefit has a cost: a wide tail interval conceals detail within that region, even when its area correctly represents a quarter of the sample.\n\nUse scientific or instrumental boundaries where they answer the question better, disclose all edges, and keep compatible edges for group comparisons. Data-dependent boundaries are an authored choice based on the dataset, not a grid that changes for each observation."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-histogram-lab cd:narration "The histogram R exercise uses the same 100 two-cluster responses and explicit common edges. Run it in the presentation, inspect the numerical bin counts and the real ggplot output, then change bin width from 4 to 40 a.u.\n\nThe counts must still sum to 100, while the two-region structure disappears at the coarser width. The editable source is retained here for reading and printing; a successful plot should not replace checking that every observation was counted."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-ecdf-question cd:narration "Histogram width and origin are representation choices: they can change the picture without changing any measurement. The empirical cumulative distribution function, ECDF, offers a bin-free view of the observed frequency distribution. It requires neither a smoothing bandwidth nor an assumed distribution family.\n\nIts essential distinction is cumulative counting. At each threshold it reports the fraction accumulated at or below that value, rather than a local bar height. The same dataset determines the same counting curve, but this objectivity does not remove sampling bias or measurement limitations."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-ecdf-concept cd:narration "For any threshold, the ECDF averages indicators that equal one when an observation is at or below that threshold and zero otherwise. Each observation contributes, including every tie. Sorting makes the calculation convenient but does not alter the data.\n\nAt an observed value the curve rises by that value's multiplicity divided by sample size, then remains constant until the next observed value. It is right-continuous: the jump is included at its observed value. Below the minimum it is zero; at and above the maximum it is one."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-ecdf-worked cd:narration "The seven-value hand example contains 2, 4, 4, 5, 5, 6 and 7 mg/L. At 3.9 mg/L, only one observation is included, giving 1/7. At 4 mg/L both tied fours enter, giving 3/7; at 4.1 mg/L the fraction remains 3/7.\n\nThe jump at four is therefore 2/7. Filled endpoints belong to the function; open endpoints show excluded left limits. Vertical connectors mark jumps and should not be read as intermediate function values."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-ecdf-river cd:narration "The river application contains 50 constructed concentrations from 0.5 to 25 mg/L in steps of 0.5. At 12.3 mg/L the ECDF includes 24 values, an observed fraction of 48%. There are no observations between 12 and 12.5, so the curve remains flat there.\n\nAt 12.5 mg/L the fraction rises to 25/50. An interval of the form $(a,b]$ has fraction $F_n(b)-F_n(a)$. These are exact sample counts, not regulatory conclusions or population estimates with guaranteed precision."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-ecdf-shapes cd:narration "The four synthetic response series share mean 50 a.u. and sample SD 10 a.u., but their cumulative patterns differ. The uniform series rises nearly steadily across its range. The normal-like series rises more quickly near its centre, while the right-skewed series has a rapid early rise and a long upper tail.\n\nThe two-cluster series rises in two regions separated by a plateau. A steep cumulative segment means many observations in a short measurement interval; a plateau means none in that interval. An ECDF is not a density curve."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-ecdf-crossing cd:narration "The uniform and normal-like cumulative curves cross. A series can include a larger fraction at a low threshold and a smaller fraction at a higher threshold, so a single universal ranking need not answer all threshold questions.\n\nA higher ECDF at a specified value means more observations are at or below that value; it does not mean the measurements themselves are larger. This is a descriptive comparison, not a significance test or proof of analytical superiority."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-ecdf-inverse cd:narration "Reading the ECDF backwards requires another explicit convention. For cumulative proportion 0.25 in the 50-value river sample, the smallest observed value whose cumulative fraction reaches the target is at rank 13, namely 6.5 mg/L.\n\nLinear interpolation instead uses fractional rank 13.25, giving 6.625 mg/L. The generalized inverse and the interpolated quantile answer related but different finite-sample questions. They should not be presented as numerically identical definitions; the inverse convention here concerns proportions strictly above zero and at most one."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-applied-mean cd:narration "The first population moment is the expectation $\\mu=\\operatorname{E}[X]$. It is a probability-weighted average of the random measurement variable, requiring a finite first absolute moment. The sample mean is its empirical counterpart, formed by equally weighting the observed values. It has the measurement unit and need not equal the unknown population expectation.\n\nThe constructed examples share a sample mean of 50 a.u., although their shapes differ. A mean locates a balancing point rather than necessarily a typical observation. Extreme values can influence it substantially, and it does not determine spread or distribution shape."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-mean-matrix cd:narration "Each row below contains the same 100 synthetic values displayed three ways: points, a boxplot with linearly interpolated quartiles and a common-bin histogram. Rows compare uniform, normal-like and strongly right-skewed samples on a common measurement axis. The cyan line marks the sample mean in every panel.\n\nThe shared mean of 50 a.u. is compatible with each different pattern. The point height serves only to separate observations visually; it is not time or another measurement. These empirical lines do not establish an unknown population mean."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-applied-variance cd:narration "The second central population moment is $\\sigma^2=\\operatorname{E}[(X-\\mu)^2]$, where $\\mu=\\operatorname{E}[X]$. It measures expected squared distance from the population centre and requires a finite second moment. An additive shift does not change it, and its unit is the squared measurement unit.\n\nFor observed data, distinguish descriptive variance with divisor $n$ from sample variance with divisor $n-1$. In the three 100-value examples these are 99 and 100 a.u. squared, respectively. The relation between these computed quantities does not make either one the known population variance."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-variance-matrix cd:narration "The three observed shapes have the same descriptive variance even though their central occupancy and tails differ. Each panel retains the mean line and shades mean plus or minus the square root of descriptive variance. This guide is in the original measurement unit, whereas variance itself is squared.\n\nThe band is not a confidence interval and makes no universal coverage promise. The identical numerical spread across the rows demonstrates why variance cannot identify shape; the point, box and histogram views expose complementary information."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-applied-skewness cd:narration "Population skewness standardizes the third central moment: the expected signed cubic deviation divided by the population SD cubed. The population SD must be positive and the third absolute moment finite. The separate lowercase sample coefficient uses divisor-n empirical central moments and is not automatically a bias-adjusted estimator.\n\nPositive skewness means stronger positive cubic contributions; negative skewness means stronger negative contributions. The coefficient is dimensionless and cannot be placed as a measured value on the response axis. A zero coefficient alone establishes neither symmetry nor normality."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-skewness-matrix cd:narration "The uniform and normal-like constructed samples have descriptive skewness near zero, while the right-skewed sample has approximately 1.759. The cyan mean line and dashed violet median line provide positional context; in the skewed row the mean exceeds the median.\n\nIn the point panels, larger points indicate larger cubic-contribution magnitudes, with colour distinguishing negative and positive deviations. The numerical coefficient summarizes the signed balance, not a tail probability or an explanation of its analytical cause."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-applied-kurtosis cd:narration "Population kurtosis is the expectation of the fourth power of the population-centred deviation divided by the fourth power of population SD. It requires a finite fourth moment and positive SD. Subtracting three gives population excess kurtosis, relative to the normal-population benchmark.\n\nThe displayed empirical counterpart uses fourth and second central moments computed with divisor $n$. It is a dimensionless descriptive coefficient, not a peak-height measurement, mode count or exact tail probability. A finite sample calculation does not establish existence of the corresponding population moment."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-kurtosis-matrix cd:narration "The uniform, normal-like and right-skewed constructed samples have descriptive kurtosis approximately 1.800, 2.834 and 6.662. Their equal variance does not imply equal fourth-power deviation weight. Larger points emphasize relative contributions within each row.\n\nThe band at two divisor-n SDs from the mean is a visual scale guide only. It is not a fixed tail-probability interval. Inspect the actual extreme observations and their origins because a few readings can dominate a fourth-power summary."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-skew-zero cd:narration "An exact counterexample separates zero skewness from symmetry. Consider 50 residuals: five equal to minus three, twenty-five equal to minus one, and twenty equal to plus two a.u. Their mean and third central moment are both zero, while the second central moment is positive.\n\nThe sample is not mirror-symmetric: there is no matching plus-three group or minus-two group. Signed cubic contributions cancel without establishing a symmetric distribution. Inspection of the observations remains necessary even after calculating a shape coefficient."@en .
  ex:l4-scene-moment-lab cd:narration "The final base-R exercise reconstructs the same deterministic midpoint quantile grids for uniform, normal-like and right-skewed samples. It prints means, descriptive and sample variances, kurtosis and skewness, then draws three rows of points, boxplots with explicitly linearly interpolated quartiles and histograms with shared measurement edges. The metric setting selects the geometric guides.\n\nRun the code in slide view, change the offset and scale factor, and compare what changes in units or location with what remains invariant in shape. Adding an extreme observation illustrates sensitivity. The program also retains the asymmetric zero-skew counterexample and checks the relation between the two empirical variance conventions."@en .
}
